# Verification

- Cover observable production behavior changes with automated tests at the appropriate level, including contracts and failure cases.
- For reproducible defects, verify a failing test before fixing them. If a deterministic reproduction is infeasible, state why and verify the fix with the strongest practical check.
- Do not claim a test, build, or product behavior passed without running the relevant check.
- For code changes, run the affected tests and required project gates using the official PowerShell scripts before committing. For documentation-only changes, review the diff and run `git diff --check`.
- For each code slice, run the official build and the narrowest affected FastTests/IntegrationTests selection. Run the complete eligible routine solution selection once at task completion; repeat only after changes, failures, or concrete unresolved concerns.
- All official test scripts exclude `E2EIntegration`, including with `-IncludeExtended` or a supplied filter. Inventory actual test flows; keep transport-free tests in the eligible selection.
- Execute verification gates via:
  - `pwsh -File ./scripts/build.ps1` to build the solution with all analyzers and warnings-as-errors enabled.
  - `pwsh -File ./scripts/test-fast.ps1` for fast unit tests.
  - `pwsh -File ./scripts/test-integration.ps1` for routine integration tests, excluding `ExtendedIntegration`.
  - `pwsh -File ./scripts/test.ps1` for routine tests across the solution, excluding `ExtendedIntegration`.
  - `pwsh -File ./scripts/test.ps1 -IncludeExtended` for an explicitly requested complete solution gate or release verification.
- Do not run `ExtendedIntegration` as a routine per-commit gate. When changes affect source/assembly relationship navigation or shared host, workspace, symbol-analysis, or response-processing dependencies, select the relevant extended tests through `scripts/test-integration.ps1 -IncludeExtended -Filter ...`. Start with the narrowest affected selection; do not automatically run the entire extended suite.
- Use the scripts' `-Filter` parameter for test selection. A filter without `-IncludeExtended` still excludes extended tests. See [Build and Tests](../../docs/development/build-and-tests.md#e2e-and-extended-integration-tests) for categories and commands.
- All scripts stream their console output into static log files under `temp/*.log` (`temp/build.log`, `temp/test-fast.log`, `temp/test-integration.log`, `temp/test.log`). When diagnosing build or test failures, inspect the corresponding log in `temp/`.
- Do not run performance tests as a routine per-commit gate; run them when the relevant acceptance criteria require them.
- For manual inspection of actual MCP tool output, add or run an on-demand scenario using [Manual MCP Exploration](../../docs/development/mcp-exploration.md). Inspect the saved output for usefulness; these experiments do not replace verification tests and must not run in routine tests or CI.

## Slow or stalled tests

- Treat an individual test taking more than five minutes, or a running verification stage making no concrete progress for five minutes, as a suspected stall that requires separate handling. Include fixture setup, workspace loading and teardown. A suite may exceed five minutes while individual tests continue to finish; polling and repeated identical log lines do not count as progress. If per-test progress is unavailable, treat five minutes without observable progress as the trigger and identify the active test/stage during diagnosis.
- Monitor active test runs at intervals of at most one minute, tracking elapsed time and available test/stage progress so the threshold is noticed during execution.
- At that threshold, capture the filter, elapsed time, last progress and available diagnostic evidence; stop only the run and child processes owned by this agent. Preserve its logs/TRX under ignored local artifacts before another official script overwrites them. Report the suspected stall promptly; never claim the interrupted gate passed.
- Immediately open or reuse a dedicated executable diagnosis/correction point in the current task roadmap, with reproduction, acceptance and verification. In an orchestrated workflow, the orchestrator must assign this point before the next implementation point. Diagnose the smallest affected test or class through the official scripts. Do not continue unrelated feature work or rerun the same broad selection until the stall is addressed; do not defer it to a later milestone.
- Do not mask the stall by increasing timeouts, skipping tests, weakening assertions, changing categories or blindly disabling parallelism. A legitimately slow test still requires separate triage: document evidence of actual progress, the reason for its expected duration and a finite time limit before resuming it. Only after that separate triage may its verified finite limit replace the five-minute default for that exact test on subsequent runs; do not generalize it to a whole suite or category. A timeout alone is neither a root cause nor a correction.
- Close the dedicated point only with evidence of a corrected cause or a verified, bounded slow-test explanation and a completed affected verification selection. Otherwise keep the finding and required gate open, report the concrete blocker and stop dependent work. Apply the existing failing-regression and code-verification rules to corrections; use targeted retries only when new evidence or a change justifies them.
